# Is the QALY fair - and should the number decide?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical","b":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical"}

chapter: "The case against the QALY"

## Two patients, one threshold
voice: female
level: basic
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    title: Two patients, one threshold
    x_label: Incremental effect (ΔE, QALYs)
    y_label: Incremental cost (ΔC, million kr)
    wtp_threshold: 0.275
    quadrant_labels: false
    points:
      - {label: Nusinersen (SMA), effect: 5, cost: 20, emphasize: true}
      - {label: Hip replacement, effect: 2, cost: 0.12}

Over six figures we'll ask whether the QALY is fair — and whether a number should ever be the thing that decides. It starts with two patients and one line.
    draw title x_axis y_axis

The first: a child with spinal muscular atrophy, treated with nusinersen. Illustrative list-price figures — roughly five quality-adjusted life years gained, at something like twenty million kroner.
    draw pt_0 pt_label_0

    pause 0.3

And here's the line we judge it against: Norway's benchmark of about 275,000 kroner per good life year, lifted for severity — up to around three times that in the most severe cases.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label

Even at that most generous, severity-weighted version of the line, this point sits about five times above it.
    highlight pt_0 effect glow

Though — well, the number the public argues about isn't the number in the room: Norway's negotiated price is confidential.
    point at.ref pt_label_0 gesture underline delivery soft

    pause 0.3

Now the second patient: a hip replacement. Same axes, same line… and, hmm, where is it? Practically sitting on the origin.
    draw pt_1 pt_label_1

Zoom in six times and there it is — about two life years gained for roughly a hundred and twenty thousand kroner, sixty thousand per life year.
    camera center.ref pt_1 zoom 6

The threshold now runs well above it: cleared four times over, by the patient nobody writes about.
    point at.ref wtp_line gesture underline

Pull back out, and the asymmetry is the lecture: at a scale that shows one patient, the other vanishes — one misses the line by a factor of fifteen, the other clears it by four.
    camera reset true

    pause 0.5

    clear

So which of the two is the unfair one? Norway has answered — twice. In October 2017, Beslutningsforum said no to nusinersen.
    text v_no "October 2017:  NO" #b5482e x 270 y 600 font_size 32

Four months later, the same forum said yes — for patients under eighteen.
    text v_yes "February 2018:  YES" #2f6b8f x 730 y 600 font_size 32
    text v_yes_sub "— patients under 18" #2f6b8f x 730 y 555 font_size 22

Between those meetings a price was renegotiated and an age limit appeared. The biology didn't change, and neither did the structure of the sum.
    text v_change "What changed: a negotiated price, and an age limit." x 500 y 470 font_size 25
    text v_change2 "Not the biology. Not the structure of the sum." #8f887c x 500 y 428 font_size 25

    pause 0.3

One reading of that: the arithmetic shuts out the rarest patients by construction — few patients, enormous per-patient cost, no ratio that can ever clear.
    text claim_a "“It shuts out the rarest.”" #b5482e x 255 y 330 font_size 24

The other: it shuts out nobody. It just makes visible the patients whose care gets displaced whenever we say yes.
    text claim_b "“It only shows who pays.”" #2f6b8f x 745 y 330 font_size 24

This is genuinely unresolved. In the United States it was settled by statute rather than by economics: federal law bars Medicare from using a cost-per-QALY threshold in coverage decisions — a disability-rights argument written into law.
    highlight claim_a claim_b effect glow

But there's a third claim in the room, and it's the one this series is really about — that the number decided.
    text claim_c "“The number decided.”" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 200 font_size 30

The record rules that one out. The same kind of ratio produced both answers; people, meeting twice, produced the difference.
    mark strike_c target claim_c kind strike
    delivery grave

    pause 0.5

So the question isn't quite whether the ratio is fair. It's what the ratio is for — and who answers for everything it leaves out.
    clear delivery brisk

Figure two takes the discrimination charge seriously: age, disability, chronic illness, and what the weights do to them. Figure three asks what we did before the QALY — fairer, or merely less visible?
    text r_head "What the next five figures ask" x 500 y 670 font_size 30
    text r1 "2 · Does it discriminate by construction?" x 500 y 580 font_size 24
    text r2 "3 · What were we doing before it?" x 500 y 510 font_size 24

Figure four is where the arithmetic genuinely breaks: the rule of rescue, ultra-rare disease, the identified life we can name against the statistical lives we can't. Figure five asks whether multi-criteria analysis and deliberation repair that — or just move the judgement somewhere less examined.
    text r3 "4 · Rescue, rarity, the identified life" x 500 y 440 font_size 24
    text r4 "5 · Does MCDA repair it — or relocate it?" x 500 y 370 font_size 24

And figure six lands where you'll be standing: if the analysis is a decision aid and not a decision machine, what exactly is the analyst accountable for?
    text r5 "6 · Aid, not machine: what the analyst owes" x 500 y 300 font_size 24

Here's the promise and its limit: nothing in the next five figures will tell you whether the child or the hip should win.
    text r_close "The number frames the choice. It does not make it." x 500 y 175 font_size 28
    mark r_box target r_close kind box

What they will tell you is this — the threshold line drew the question, twenty million kroner against a hundred and twenty thousand, and the people in the room, twice, drew the answer.
    delivery grave

## Where next
end_page: true

    link end_again href lecture:20 title "Watch again" form text mark replay open here x 500 y 400 font_size 28
    text end_count "20 of 20" x 500 y 330 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
